###Setting up a proportion

Corresponding sides of similar triangles are proportional, so we can set up and solve a proportion:

$\dfrac{LM}{LP}=\dfrac{NM}{OP}$

Side $\overline{LM}$ is equal to $\overline{LP}+\blueE{\overline{PM}}$ and we need to find the length of $\blueE{\overline{PM}}$.

We picked side $\overline{LP}$ because it corresponds to side ${\overline{LM}}$.

Then we picked another pair of corresponding sides whose lengths we already knew.

$\begin{array}{rcl}
&\triangle LMN
\\
\text{hypotenuse}
&\overbrace{\underbrace{\!\!\!\!\!\left\{ 
\dfrac{LP +\blueE{PM}}{LP}=\dfrac{NM}{OP}
\right\}\!\!\!\!\!}}
&{\text{long} \atop \text{leg}}
\\
&\triangle LPO
\end{array}$
